    "@type": "Question",
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: item.a },
  })),
};

const chipLabel = { high: "Scalable", mid: "Scalable with a business layer", low: "Not so scalable" };
---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  seoTitle={seoTitle}
  seoDescription={seoDescription}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="career-options"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>After failing Class 12 in India, your career options depend first on which result you
    have: one or two subjects usually means a supplementary exam, and a wider fail means a repeat
    year, NIOS, or a diploma or ITI that asks only for Class 10.</strong> None of these closes the door
    to a degree or a good income. The decisions that unlock high income opportunities come next: pick
    one lane and build a high-income skill portfolio people can see.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Find your exact result first.</strong> Compartment, essential repeat and a failed practical lead to different next steps, and form deadlines pass quickly.</li>
      <li><strong>You have four routes.</strong> Supplementary exam, NIOS, a diploma or ITI using Class 10, or skills first with a bridge back to study. Choose by result, money and how you learn.</li>
      <li><strong>Next step:</strong> tell one trusted adult, get your marksheet read properly, and finish one real project in your lane. It starts your high-income skill portfolio and a faster path to financial freedom.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    This guide is part of our set of
    <a href="/blog/career-options/">career options for every stream and degree</a>. If you want to see
    what is possible with a clear pass, read
    <a href="/blog/career-options/career-options-after-12th/">career options after 12th</a>. For Class 10, see <a href="/blog/career-options/what-to-do-after-failing-class-10-india/">what to do after failing Class 10</a>.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="which-result">First, which result do you have?</h2>

  <p>
    "Failed" covers four different situations. The next step depends on which one you are in.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Your result</th>
          <th>What the rules say</th>
          <th>Next move</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {resultRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Your result"><strong>{row.result}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="What the rules say">{row.means}</td>
            <td data-label="Next move">{row.next}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Checked on 30 October 2026 against the Delhi High Court judgment in Md Aram v. State of NCT of
    Delhi (9 July 2025), which quotes the CBSE circular and bye-laws, and press reports for 2026 dates.
    Circulars change every year, so confirm with your school.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      A failed result is a data point, not a verdict on you. Most students who lose a year do not lose
      their career, but they do lose time if they wait. Choose a route in the first few weeks, even if it
      is a small one.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="four-routes">Four ways forward</h2>

  <div class="ladder">
    {fourRoutes.map((r) => (
      <div class="ladder-step">
        <div class="ladder-step__num">{r.n}</div>
        <div>
          <div class="ladder-step__title">{r.title}</div>
          <p>{r.body}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="supplementary">The supplementary exam and the repeat year</h2>

  <h3>When it makes sense</h3>

  <p>
    If you failed one or two subjects and the rest of your result is solid, the supplementary exam is
    usually the fastest way back. You keep your batch for college admissions, and you stop the loss at
    one subject.
  </p>

  <h3>What to ask your school</h3>

  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>Is my result compartment, essential repeat or something else?</li>
    <li>Which component did I fail: theory, practical or internal marks?</li>
    <li>What are the form deadline and the exam date this year?</li>
    <li>Can I attend remedial classes for the subject?</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>When to repeat the year</h3>

  <p>
    Repeat if you failed several subjects and need structured teaching and a fixed routine. Change
    something concrete: the teacher, the study method or the stream, so the second try is not a copy of
    the first.
  </p>

  <h2 id="nios">NIOS: the open school route</h2>

  <p>
    The National Institute of Open Schooling lets you study at your own pace and take on-demand exams.
    Its FAQ gives these rules.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Topic</th>
          <th>What the NIOS FAQ says</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {nios.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Topic"><strong>{row.fact}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="What the NIOS FAQ says">{row.detail}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    From a NIOS regional centre FAQ page, read on 30 October 2026. Fees, ages and deadlines are updated
    often, so check the current table on the NIOS site before you apply.
  </p>

  <h2 id="diploma-iti">Diploma and ITI through your Class 10 certificate</h2>

  <p>
    Diplomas and ITI trades ask for Class 10, so a failed Class 12 does not block them. Rules vary by
    state, and this table shows what published sources say.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Path</th>
          <th>Entry rule in the sources</th>
          <th>Where it leads</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {diplomaRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Path"><strong>{row.path}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Entry rule in the sources">{row.entry}</td>
            <td data-label="Where it leads">{row.leads}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    ITI trades on the DGT page include Electronics Mechanic, Computer Operator and Programming
    Assistant, Cosmetology, Dress Making, Secretarial Practice and an Artificial Intelligence
    Programming Assistant course. Read the full trade list for your state, as it is longer than the
    sample on that page.
  </p>

  <h2 id="bridge">A bridge to a degree without Class 12</h2>

  <p>
    If you want a degree later, ask about open-university bridge programmes. A study-centre page for the
    IGNOU Bachelor's Preparatory Programme lists no formal qualification requirement and a minimum age
    of 18, with a duration of six months to two years, and says the B.Com listing accepts it in place of
    Class 12.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is one centre's description, so confirm the current rules with IGNOU. Check also whether
    the employer or entrance exam you want accepts the bridge route.
  </p>

  <h2 id="routes">Eight career lanes, strongest growth first</h2>

  <p>
    These lanes are ordered by how far proof of skill can lift income, with the rank-based lane last.
    Each card names who pays, the main hurdle and the next move.
  </p>

  <div class="route-grid">
    {routes.map((r) => (
      <div class="route-card">
        <div class="route-card__top">
          <span class="route-card__n">{r.n}</span>
          <span class={`chip chip--${r.scale}`}>{chipLabel[r.scale]}</span>
        </div>
        <h3>{r.name}</h3>
        <p class="route-card__why">{r.why}</p>
        <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>Who pays you</span>{r.pays}</p>
        <p class="route-row route-row--violet"><span>What earns trust</span>{r.trust}</p>
        <p class="route-row route-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>{r.watch}</p>
        <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>How it grows</span>{r.grow}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>The ownership path:</strong> a small service in your own area. A repair counter, a
      tuition class, an online store or a small design service starts with a few customers and costs
      little to test.
    </p>
    <p>
      Write the price and what is included for every job. Keep a list of your customers, and add a
      helper when you have more orders than hours.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="two-stage">Lanes that filter you twice</h2>

  <p>
    Some lanes ask you to clear two separate gates. Plan for both.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Lane</th>
          <th>Stage 1: the entry filter</th>
          <th>Stage 2: the second filter</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {stageRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Lane"><strong>{row.route}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Stage 1: the entry filter">{row.first}</td>
            <td data-label="Stage 2: the second filter">{row.second}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="parents">Talking to your family</h2>

  <p>
    Come with a plan, not an apology. Parents worry most when there is no plan.
  </p>

  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>Say the exact result: which subjects, which marks.</li>
    <li>Show the earliest supplementary or NIOS date.</li>
    <li>Offer two options with costs, such as supplementary plus a small project, or NIOS plus a skill course.</li>
    <li>Ask for one thing: help with the form fee or someone to review the plan.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="money">Money and fee traps</h2>

  <p>
    Keep the degree fee near a tenth of the family's total education budget, as a rule of thumb and not
    a research finding. After a failed year, the cheapest legal route usually wins: a supplementary form,
    an on-demand NIOS exam or a government ITI.
  </p>

  <p>
    Be careful with centres that promise a pass, a fast-track certificate or direct college admission for
    a fee. Check every claim against the board's circular and the NIOS site, and keep receipts.
  </p>

  <p>
    Free learning is useful for skills. Judge each course by the teacher's recent work, the practice it
    gives and the feedback you get, and finish a project before you pay for a second course.
  </p>

  <p class="note-line">
    We give no salary figures because we found no dependable source for these lanes. Read live job ads
    and ask people in the work.
  </p>

  <h2 id="checkpoint">The 4-Checkpoint Protocol for your lane</h2>

  <p>
    Before a lane takes a year or more, test it with <strong>The 4-Checkpoint Protocol</strong>:
    Biology, Context, Market and Survival.
  </p>

  <div class="protocol">
    {protocolItems.map((item) => (
      <div class="protocol-row">
        <div class="protocol-row__num">{item.num}</div>
        <div>
          <div class="protocol-row__title">{item.title}</div>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="gates">The 3 Gates: proof before commitment</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>The 3 Gates</strong> check whether your effort turns into work other people rely on. Run one
    small project through all three before you commit to a long course.
  </p>

  <div class="gates-block">
    <div class="gates-row">
      {gateItems.map((item) => (
        <div class="gate">
          <span class="gate__num">{item.label}</span>
          <span class="gate__name">{item.title}</span>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="mistakes">Expensive mistakes</h2>

  <div class="mistake-list">
